      "output": "Water is a chemical substance with the chemical formula **$H_2O$**. It is composed of two hydrogen atoms bonded to one oxygen atom.\n\nHere is a breakdown of what makes water unique and important:\n\n**1. Chemical Structure**\n*   **Composition:** It consists of two parts hydrogen and one part oxygen.\n*   **Polarity:** The oxygen atom is slightly more electronegative (attracts electrons) than the hydrogen atoms. This gives water a \"polar\" molecular shape, meaning one end of the molecule has a slight negative charge and the other has a slight positive charge. This polarity is what allows water to stick to itself and dissolve other substances.\n\n**2. States of Matter**\nWater is one of the few substances on Earth that naturally exists as a solid, liquid, and gas at temperatures commonly found on the planet:\n*   **Solid (Ice):** When frozen, water molecules form a rigid, crystalline structure.\n*   **Liquid:** At room temperature, water flows and takes the shape of its container.\n*   **Gas (Steam/Water Vapor):** When heated, water molecules move so fast they escape into the air as a gas.\n\n**3. Unique Physical Properties**\n*   **Universal Solvent:** Because of its polarity, water can dissolve more substances than any other liquid. This is why it is essential for transporting nutrients and waste in living organisms.\n*   **High Surface Tension:** Water molecules stick together strongly at the surface, creating a kind of \"skin.\" This allows small insects to walk on water.\n*   **High Specific Heat Capacity:** Water can absorb a lot of heat before it gets hot, and it releases a lot of heat before it freezes. This helps regulate the Earth's climate and keeps the human body at a stable temperature.\n\n**4. Biological Importance**\nWater is the foundation of life. It is the primary component of all known forms of life and is necessary for:\n*   Cellular reactions and metabolism.\n*   Transporting nutrients and oxygen through the blood.\n*   Lubricating joints and organs.\n*   Regulating body temperature through sweating and panting."
